-# Six-fiber exact kissing configurations (arXiv:1301.4884)
-
-> **Status:** the generator and every local gate pass, but the required four-vendor
-> hardness and G9 arms are **not complete**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 "Key limit
-> exceeded" on every redraw. The module is therefore not yet submission-ready, and
-> the current `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` is provider-error evidence, not a hardness
-> result.
-
-| profile field | value |
-|---|---|
-| Track | **B** -- no-tool compression |
-| native domain | geometry |
-| object regime | rational exact |
-| computational core | CSP/SAT over geometric candidates |
-| certificate | 6-by-4 rational matrix |
-| intended intuition | symmetry: one common Hopf-fiber phase |
-| domain essentiality | native |
-| reduction | none |
-
-## What the problem is
-
-[Altschuler and Pérez-Garrido's paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.4884) represents
-the four-dimensional 24-cell as four points on each of six Hopf fibers. Equation
-(1) gives the Hopf map, Equation (3) its fibers, and Equations (4)--(7) translate
-kissing distance into phase separation. Immediately after Equation (7), the paper
-observes that adding one common phase to all fibers preserves a kissing configuration.
-
-An instance lists rational representatives of several quarter-turn orbits on each
-of the six fibers. The solver returns one representative per fiber. The checker
-expands each by `J(x1,x2,x3,x4)=(-x2,x1,-x4,x3)` and verifies, with exact rational
-arithmetic, that all 24 vectors are unit vectors and every inner product is at most
-`1/2`. It accepts any valid selection and never reads the planted answer.
-
